desktop-shell-login-greeter(1)

# NAME

desktop-shell-login-greeter - graphical login screen for the greetd login daemon

# SYNOPSIS

*desktop-shell-login-greeter* [*-s* _FILE_]

# DESCRIPTION

*desktop-shell-login-greeter* asks for a username and password, lets the user
pick a session, and shows every further question or message from PAM.
Escape abandons a question or a running login attempt.

It draws one wlr-layer-shell surface on every output. The login form is shown
on the output the pointer last entered, and only that surface takes keyboard
input.

It exits with status 0 once greetd has accepted the session to start, and
with a non-zero status otherwise.

# OPTIONS

*-h, --help*
	Show the help message and exit.

*-s, --style* _FILE_
	Load _FILE_ instead of the shipped login-greeter.css. The shared theme is
	still loaded first.

# SESSIONS

Sessions are read from the desktop entries in _@session_entries_directory@_.
Entries with _Hidden_ or _NoDisplay_ set, or whose _TryExec_ program is
missing, are skipped. A session starts with _XDG_SESSION_TYPE=wayland_ and
with _XDG_CURRENT_DESKTOP_ set from the entry's _DesktopNames_.

# SETUP

The greeter needs a compositor that supports wlr-layer-shell, such as
*sway*(1), and the compositor must exit once the greeter does. The installed
_/etc/greetd/login-greeter-compositor-configuration_ does this. Point greetd
at it in _/etc/greetd/config.toml_:

```
[default_session]
command = "sway --config /etc/greetd/login-greeter-compositor-configuration"
```

There is deliberately no graphical fallback, as a terminal on the login
screen could hand out a shell. If the greeter fails, log in on another
virtual terminal, for example with Ctrl+Alt+F2.

# STYLING

Style sheets load in this order, each able to override the ones before:
_@shared_data_directory@/shared-theme.css_, _@shared_data_directory@/login-greeter.css_
(or the *--style* file), then _shared-theme.css_ and _login-greeter.css_ from
_$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/desktop-shell-components_ if present.

The windows have the _login-greeter-background_ class. The form is a box named
_login-form-card_ holding labels with the _login-form-field-label_ class,
fields with the _login-form-field_ class, a label named _login-form-message_
(with the _error_ class for errors) and a button named
_login-form-submit-button_.

# SEE ALSO

*greetd*(1), *greetd*(5), *greetd-ipc*(7), *sway*(1)
